{
  "id": "al1",
  "title": "Bits and Switches",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "al1-l1",
      "title": "On and Off",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "January 1975: the magazine Popular Electronics puts a computer kit on its cover, the MITS Altair 8800. It costs $439, and it has no keyboard and no screen. Just a row of toggle switches and red lights.",
        "Each switch is a bit: down is 0, up is 1. Each light shows a bit the same way: off is 0, on is 1.",
        "The switches have values, from the right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128. Each one is worth twice as much as the one to its right. Add the values of the switches that are up, and you have the number."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
          "00 000 101   ; the value 5",
          "11 010 011   ; OUT",
          "11 111 111   ; port 377: the lights",
          "01 110 110   ; HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○○○○●○● = 5"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e1",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 1.",
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Only the rightmost switch (D0, worth 1) is up."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e2",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 8.",
          "answer": 8,
          "explanation": "Switch D3 is worth 8."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is switch D4 worth?",
          "options": [
            "16",
            "4",
            "8",
            "32"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "1, 2, 4, 8, 16: D4 is the fifth from the right."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e4",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 3.",
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "3 = 2 + 1: switches D1 and D0."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which number do the lights show? ○○○○○●●○",
          "options": [
            "6",
            "3",
            "110",
            "12"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "D2 (4) and D1 (2) are on: 4 + 2 = 6."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does a bit hold?",
          "options": [
            "0 or 1",
            "A number from 0 to 9",
            "A letter",
            "Eight values"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Off or on, down or up."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al1-l2",
      "title": "Counting in Binary",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Eight bits make a byte. With all switches down it's 0, with all up it's 128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 255. So a byte holds 256 different values.",
        "To turn a number into switches, start on the left: does 128 fit? Then that switch goes up and you subtract 128. Then try 64, 32, and so on.",
        "Example: 100 = 64 + 32 + 4, so D6, D5 and D2 go up: 01100100."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
          "01 100 100   ; the value 100",
          "11 010 011   ; OUT",
          "11 111 111   ; port 377: the lights",
          "01 110 110   ; HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○●●○○●○○ = 100"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e1",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 10.",
          "answer": 10,
          "explanation": "10 = 8 + 2: D3 and D1."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e2",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 255.",
          "answer": 255,
          "explanation": "All eight switches up: the biggest value of a byte."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 10000000 in decimal?",
          "options": [
            "128",
            "1",
            "10000000",
            "256"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Only D7 is up, and it's worth 128."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 13 in binary?",
          "options": [
            "00001101",
            "00001011",
            "00010011",
            "00001110"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "13 = 8 + 4 + 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e5",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "00001111 is ___",
          "code": [
            "00001111 = ___"
          ],
          "options": [
            "15",
            "16",
            "8",
            "255"
          ],
          "answer": "15",
          "explanation": "8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 15."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l2-e6",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to 100.",
          "answer": 100,
          "explanation": "100 = 64 + 32 + 4."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al1-l3",
      "title": "Your First Program",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "On the Altair, a program is a list of bytes in memory. You set the switches to a byte and flip DEPOSIT, then the next byte and DEPOSIT NEXT, and so on. Then RUN.",
        "This program has five bytes. 00 111 110 means \"put the next byte into register A\" (MVI A). 11 010 011 with 11 111 111 means \"show A on the lights\" (OUT 377). 01 110 110 stops the processor (HLT).",
        "In this app, you write one byte per line. Everything after ; is a comment. Change the second byte, and the lights show something else."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
          "10 101 010   ; the pattern to show",
          "11 010 011   ; OUT",
          "11 111 111   ; port 377: the lights",
          "01 110 110   ; HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ●○●○●○●○ = 170"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does 01 110 110 do?",
          "options": [
            "Stops the processor (HLT)",
            "Shows A on the lights",
            "Puts a value into A",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "HLT: halt. Every program ends with it."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e2",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to the first byte of the program: MVI A (00 111 110).",
          "answer": 62,
          "explanation": "00 111 110 is 62, the MVI A instruction."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Which byte makes the lights show 00000011?",
          "code": [
            "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
            "___",
            "11 010 011   ; OUT",
            "11 111 111   ; port 377",
            "01 110 110   ; HLT"
          ],
          "options": [
            "00 000 011",
            "00 011 110",
            "11 000 000",
            "11 111 111"
          ],
          "answer": "00 000 011",
          "explanation": "The byte after MVI A is the value that ends up on the lights."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e4",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the five bytes in the right order.",
          "lines": [
            "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
            "00 000 111   ; 7",
            "11 010 011   ; OUT",
            "11 111 111   ; port 377",
            "01 110 110   ; HLT"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
            "00 000 111   ; 7",
            "11 010 011   ; OUT",
            "11 111 111   ; port 377",
            "01 110 110   ; HLT"
          ],
          "explanation": "MVI A and its value, OUT and its port, then HLT."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't load. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "00 111 110   ; MVI A",
            "00 000 11   ; 3",
            "11 010 011   ; OUT",
            "11 111 111   ; port 377",
            "01 110 110   ; HLT"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "That byte has only 7 bits. Every byte needs exactly 8 switches."
        },
        {
          "id": "al1-l3-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Who wrote the first BASIC for the Altair, in 1975?",
          "options": [
            "Bill Gates and Paul Allen",
            "Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak",
            "Kemeny and Kurtz",
            "Ritchie and Thompson"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Altair BASIC was the first product of their new company, Micro-Soft."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
